      content: '**Cancer Pain Classification (Essential Framework)**:\n- **Nociceptive somatic pain**: Abdominal wall involvement, peritoneal carcinomatosis causing diffuse abdominal aching\n- **Nociceptive visceral pain**: Pancreatic tumor mass effect, hepatic capsular distension from metastases — deep, poorly localized, gnawing quality\n- **Neuropathic pain**: Celiac plexus invasion — burning, shooting, lancinating quality in left flank and back. May involve splanchnic nerves and retroperitoneal nerve plexus.\n- **Cancer-related bone pain**: Assess for osseous metastases (not yet identified but must be surveilled)\n- **Treatment-related pain**: Chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy from oxaliplatin component of FOLFIRINOX\n\n**WHO Analgesic Ladder (Modified for Cancer Pain)**:\n- Step 1: Non-opioids (acetaminophen, NSAIDs)\n- Step 2: Weak opioids (tramadol, codeine) — often bypassed in moderate-severe cancer pain\n- Step 3: Strong opioids (morphine, oxycodone, hydromorphone, fentanyl, methadone)\n- Step 4 (modern addition): Interventional procedures (nerve blocks, intrathecal pumps, neurolysis)\n- **Adjuvant agents at every step**: Antidepressants, anticonvulsants, corticosteroids, bisphosphonates\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **ECOG Performance Status**: Currently 2 (ambulatory, capable of self-care, unable to carry out work activities, up and about >50% of waking hours)\n- **Karnofsky Performance Scale**: 60% (requires occasional assistance but cares for most personal needs)\n- **Edmonton Symptom Assessment System (ESAS)**: Pain 8/10, fatigue 7/10, nausea 5/10, appetite 2/10, drowsiness 6/10, depression 5/10\n- **Nutritional status**: Significant cancer cachexia, albumin 2.8 g/dL, prealbumin 12 mg/dL\n- **Gait and mobility**: Walks with rolling walker, limited to 150 feet before needing to rest. Difficulty with stairs (bedroom and bathroom are on second floor).\n- **ADLs**: Independent in basic ADLs with modified technique but reports increasing fatigue 