      content: '**Opioid Rotation — Rationale and Method**:\nThis patient has a poor response to morphine with significant side effects (drowsiness) — classic indication for opioid rotation due to incomplete cross-tolerance between opioid receptor subtypes.\n\n**Current Opioid Burden**:\n- ER morphine 60 mg BID = 120 mg/day\n- IR morphine 15 mg x 5 doses average = 75 mg/day\n- Total oral morphine equivalent = 195 mg/day\n\n**Rotation Options**:\n1. **Hydromorphone**: Convert using equianalgesic table (oral morphine 30 mg = oral hydromorphone 6 mg). 195 mg morphine = 39 mg hydromorphone. Apply 25-50% dose reduction for incomplete cross-tolerance = 20-30 mg oral hydromorphone daily. Consider ER hydromorphone (Exalgo) with IR hydromorphone for breakthrough.\n2. **Methadone**: Potent for neuropathic pain (NMDA antagonist properties), long half-life, inexpensive. However, complex pharmacokinetics and QTc prolongation risk. Conversion ratios are NOT linear — for >200 mg oral morphine, ratio is approximately 8-12:1. Requires inpatient monitoring for initiation. Consider if neuropathic component dominant.\n3. **Transdermal fentanyl**: Useful if oral route compromised (nausea, dysphagia, bowel obstruction). Convert: oral morphine 195 mg/day divided by 2 = approximately 100 mcg/hr patch. Apply 25% reduction = 75 mcg/hr. Provide IR breakthrough (oral morphine or fentanyl lozenge).\n\n**Recommended rotation**: Hydromorphone given her renal function is adequate, nausea concerns favor ER formulation, and it has less neuroexcitatory metabolite accumulation (no M3G equivalent).\n\n**Adjuvant Medications**:\n- **Neuropathic component**: Add pregabalin 75 mg BID, titrate to 150 mg BID (better bioavailability than gabapentin and less pill burden in polypharmacy)\n- **Continue dexamethasone**: Reduces peritumoral edema, improves appetite, anti-emetic properties\n- **Acetaminophen 650 mg QID**: Additive analgesia with opioids, opioid-sparing\n- **Consider ketamine**: Sub-anesthetic oral ketamine 10-25 mg TID for refractory neuropathic pain (off-label but used in palliative care)\n\n**Celiac Plexus Block/Neurolysis**:\n- **Indication**: Visceral abdominal pain from pancreatic cancer unresponsive to pharmacologic management — this is one of the strongest indications in all of interventional pain medicine\n- **Technique**: CT or fluoroscopy-guided, posterior transcrural or anterior approach. Diagnostic block with local anesthetic first; if >50% relief, proceed with neurolysis using absolute alcohol or phenol.\n- **Evidence**: Randomized trials show celiac plexus neurolysis provides superior pain relief to pharmacologic management alone in pancreatic cancer, with reduced opioid requirements and improved quality of life\n- **Timing**: Should be offered early rather than as a last resort. In this patient with severe visceral pain and neuropathic features suggestive of celiac plexus involvement, this should be pursued within 1-2 weeks.\n- **Risks**: Transient diarrhea (30-40%), orthostatic hypotension (10-20%), rare but serious complications include retroperitoneal hemorrhage, paraplegia (artery of Adamkiewicz), pneumothorax\n- **Expected benefit**: 70-90% of pancreatic cancer patients achieve good-to-excellent pain relief lasting weeks to months',
