      title: '+ How do you design a palliative rehabilitation program that balances function with realistic prognosis?',
      content: '**Palliative Rehabilitation — Principles**:\nPalliative rehabilitation is not about "getting better" in the traditional sense. It is about maximizing function, dignity, and quality of life within the constraints of a life-limiting illness. The goal shifts from restorative to **adaptive and supportive**.\n\n**Rehabilitation Goal Framework (Time-Based)**:\n- **Short-term (2-4 weeks)**: Optimize pain control, improve home safety, maintain current ADL independence, address fall risk\n- **Medium-term (4-12 weeks)**: Build enough endurance and strength to walk her daughter down the aisle in 11 weeks. This is a concrete, meaningful, achievable goal.\n- **Long-term (ongoing)**: Maintain comfort, adapt environment as function declines, prepare for increasing dependency, advance care planning\n\n**Exercise Prescription in Advanced Cancer**:\n- **Evidence base**: Exercise in advanced cancer improves fatigue (strongest evidence), physical function, mood, and sleep. It does NOT accelerate disease progression.\n- **Aerobic training**: Start at current tolerance (she can walk 150 feet). Progress by 10% per week. Use RPE (Borg scale 3-4/10). Walking program in the home, progressing to outdoors. Target: sustained walking of 100-150 feet continuously (approximate aisle length plus standing time)\n- **Resistance training**: Light resistance (1-2 lb weights, resistance bands). Focus on sit-to-stand, hip extensors, and quadriceps for gait stability. 2-3 sets of 8-10 repetitions, 3 days per week.\n- **Balance training**: Static standing balance progressing to dynamic. Tandem stance, single-leg stance with support. Critical for fall prevention and confidence walking the aisle.\n- **Prehabilitation for the wedding**: Practice walking the expected distance in the expected shoes. Practice standing for the ceremony duration. Develop a backup plan (wheelchair nearby, seated option).\n\n**Nutritional Optimization**:\n- Dietitian consultation for cancer cachexia management\n- High-calorie, high-protein supplements (goal: 1.2-1.5 g protein/kg/day)\n- Megestrol acetate or dronabinol for appetite stimulation (though evidence is limited)\n- Address nausea aggressively to enable intake\n\n**Home Modifications**:\n- First-floor bedroom and bathroom setup (hospital bed, bedside commode) to eliminate stairs\n- Shower bench, grab bars, raised toilet seat\n- Remove throw rugs and clutter (fall prevention)\n- Rollator walker optimization — ensure correct height, consider a rollator with a seat for rest breaks\n\n**Psychosocial Support**:\n- Social work referral for advance care planning, POLST/MOLST completion, power of attorney, healthcare proxy\n- Psychology or chaplain referral for existential distress\n- Caregiver burden assessment for the daughter (who is simultaneously planning a wedding and caring for a dying parent)\n- Support groups for patients with advanced cancer\n\n**Monitoring**:\n- Weekly reassessment of ECOG status and ESAS\n- Titrate exercise intensity to current functional status (may need to scale back if disease progresses)\n- Reassess goals regularly — if prognosis changes, goals must adapt',
