      content: '**Hospice vs. Palliative Care — Critical Distinctions**:\n\n**Palliative Care**:\n- Appropriate at any stage of serious illness, regardless of prognosis\n- Can be concurrent with curative or disease-modifying treatment\n- Focuses on symptom management, quality of life, and psychosocial support\n- Provided by a specialty team (physician, NP, social worker, chaplain) in any setting\n- Covered by standard insurance as a consultative service\n- No requirement to forgo curative treatment\n\n**Hospice**:\n- A specific model of palliative care for patients with estimated prognosis of 6 months or less if the disease follows its expected course\n- Medicare Hospice Benefit requires two physician certifications of terminal prognosis\n- Patient must elect the benefit and agree to forgo curative treatment for the terminal diagnosis\n- Provides comprehensive services: nursing, aide, social work, chaplain, bereavement support, medications related to the terminal diagnosis, DME\n- Settings: home, inpatient hospice facility, nursing home, hospital\n- Can be revoked at any time if patient changes mind\n\n**Concurrent Hospice and Rehabilitation**:\n- This is a common source of confusion. The hospice benefit covers services that are **palliative in nature**, which CAN include rehabilitation.\n- **PT, OT, and SLP** can be provided under hospice if they are aimed at comfort, safety, and maintaining function (not restoration to a prior level)\n- Examples: PT for fall prevention, safe transfers, and gait training to maintain mobility; OT for energy conservation and adaptive equipment; SLP for safe swallowing\n- The key distinction is **intent**: rehabilitation under hospice must be documented as comfort-directed or safety-directed, not restorative\n- Many hospice agencies have therapists on staff or contract with rehabilitation providers\n\n**For This Patient**:\n- She meets prognostic criteria for hospice (4-8 month estimated survival, declining functional status, stopped chemotherapy)\n- However, she has a time-limited, concrete rehabilitation goal (walking down the aisle in 11 weeks)\n- **Recommended approach**: Initiate palliative care consultation now. Discuss hospice enrollment with the patient and daughter. If she enrolls in hospice, negotiate with the hospice agency for therapy services directed at her wedding goal.\n- Alternatively, she can receive outpatient rehabilitation now (covered by standard Medicare Part B) and transition to hospice when she is ready or when her function declines further.\n- Reframe for the patient: "Hospice is not giving up. It is choosing to focus all of our energy on what matters most to you — being present and comfortable for your daughter\'s wedding and the time you have together. Hospice brings more support into your home, not less."\n\n**Advance Care Planning**:\n- POLST/MOLST form completion\n- Healthcare proxy designation (likely daughter)\n- Discussion of resuscitation preferences\n- Goals of care documentation in the medical record\n- Ensure oncology, PM&R, palliative care, and primary care are aligned on the care plan',
