      content: '**Step 1 — Honor the Emotion and the Meaning**:\n"Thank you for sharing that with me. I can hear how deeply important this is to you, and I want you to know that I take this goal as seriously as you do. This is not just about walking — this is about a promise you made to your daughter, and it matters."\n\n**Step 2 — Be Honest Without Crushing Hope**:\n"I want to be completely honest with you, because you deserve that. Your body has been through a lot, and the cancer and the treatments have taken a real toll on your strength. Eleven weeks from now, I cannot guarantee exactly how you will feel. But what I can tell you is that there are things we can do right now to give you the best possible chance of being there the way you want to be."\n\n**Step 3 — Present a Concrete Plan**:\n"Here is what I am going to do. We are going to start a rehabilitation program specifically designed around that walk down the aisle. We are going to work on your endurance so you can walk the distance. We are going to work on your balance so you feel steady. We are going to get your pain under better control so you are not suffering through this. And we are going to build in rest strategies and backup plans so that no matter what happens, you are there for your daughter on that day."\n\n**Step 4 — Normalize Adaptation Without Framing It as Failure**:\n"Now, I want us to think about this together. What does \'walking her down the aisle\' look like to you? Some of my patients have done this with a walker and felt proud. Some have been in a wheelchair and held their daughter\'s hand the whole way. The love and the meaning are the same whether you are walking independently or being wheeled. What matters is that you are there, present, and as comfortable as possible."\n\n**Step 5 — Empower Shared Goal-Setting**:\n"Can we plan for the best-case scenario while also having a backup that feels okay to you? I would like to involve your daughter in this conversation too, if you are comfortable with that, so we can all work toward the same goal."\n\n**Step 6 — Address the Unspoken**:\n"I also want you to know that whatever happens in the weeks and months ahead, the work we are doing now is not just about the wedding. It is about giving you the most comfortable, meaningful time possible with your daughter and the people you love. That is worth fighting for, and I am in this with you."\n\n**Key Communication Principles**:\n- **Hope and honesty are not opposites** — you can maintain hope while being truthful about uncertainty\n- **Goal concordance**: The physician\'s treatment plan is built around the patient\'s stated goal, not abstract medical outcomes\n- **Adaptive planning**: Offering a continuum of success (walking independently, walking with a device, wheelchair participation) ensures the patient does not feel like a failure if full ambulation is not possible\n- **Avoid prognostic precision**: Do not say "you have 4-8 months" — frame uncertainty honestly without destroying hope\n- **Include the family**: The daughter is both caregiver and central to the patient\'s primary goal; her involvement is essential',
